<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">5</span>
    A roadmap to the reading
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p><strong>Part One: Ambiguity and Freedom.</strong> De Beauvoir sets up the philosophical problem. She argues that human beings are marked by a fundamental ambiguity: we are simultaneously free consciousnesses that transcend our situation and beings who are embedded in and shaped by a world we did not choose. Most ethical systems, she argues, have tried to resolve this ambiguity by emphasizing one side at the expense of the other, either reducing human beings to determined creatures of nature or treating them as pure rational wills untouched by circumstance. She insists on holding both together. The ethical task is not to escape ambiguity but to assume it honestly.</p>
<p><strong>Part Two: Personal Freedom and Others.</strong> The longer and more analytically rich section. De Beauvoir catalogs several attitudes of bad faith, that is, several characteristic ways people flee from genuine freedom into false security. The sub-man, the serious man, the nihilist, the adventurer, the passionate man: each represents a recognizable human type, and each represents a failure to live with the full weight of freedom. Against all of them she develops her account of what genuine freedom looks like, arriving at the book's central claim: that to genuinely will one's own freedom is also to will the freedom of others. This is not a sentimental claim; she develops it as a structural argument. Work through it carefully, because it is the pivot on which the whole book turns.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
